  "cellule": {
    "liftInterference": true,
    "_source": "Derived biplane lift interference (BiplaneCellule: a Biot-Savart lattice on this config's own wings; Prandtl NACA Rep. 116, Munk NACA Rep. 151 General Biplane Theory 1923). ON because clarkY-like peaks at CLmax 1.480, a true 2-D value, so the interference is not already in the table. Not a tuned number: nothing here is fitted to a stall speed."
  },
  "limits": {
    "vneMs": 44.7,
    "gMax": 3.5,
    "gMin": -1.5,
    "vaMs": 34.3858,
    "vaSource": "DERIVED 2026-11-11, not read off a manual: Va = Vs1 * sqrt(n_limit), which is the definition of the manoeuvring speed (FAR 23.335(c)) -- the speed at which full control deflection makes exactly the limit load factor. Vs1 is this model's own clean stall speed at sea level (PracticeScenario.EstimateVso, flaps 0) so the figure is INDICATED, and n_limit is this type's own limits.gMax. Capped at vneMs where the product runs past it. FinLoadProbe prints Vs1 and Va for the whole fleet and re-derives them; it was needed because Ty's 2026-11-11 fin limit load is measured AT Va and only glider-grob103-acro-like published one.",
    "finLimitLoadN": 1464.4,
    "finOrdinaryLoadN": 1032.5,
    "finLoadSource": "MEASURED 2026-11-11 by FinLoadProbe, flying Ty's manoeuvre: \"At va measure the force on the vertical tail with a yaw one way followed by an immediate yaw full deflection the other way then add 15% and call that the limit load with a 2.0 ultimate strength before any fatigue\". That is the rudder-reversal case of the yaw manoeuvring conditions (FAR 23.441 / 25.351), the case that took the fin off AA587, and it is flown as the real thing: trimmed power-off at Va (34.4 m/s IAS) at 900 m in still air, full rudder one way, then the opposite stop applied at the sideslip's overswing peak (29.1 deg, 1.52 s in) so the slip angle and the opposite deflection ADD on the fin. Peak fin+rudder lateral force: 1033 N on the single deflection, 1273 N on the reversal -- 1.23x worse, which is why the case is a reversal. finLimitLoadN = 1273 x 1.15 (Ty's 15%). The 2.0 ultimate is Ty's and is not the usual 1.5; it needs no field because OverstressMember.BreakRatio is already 2.0. finOrdinaryLoadN is the single-deflection peak and is the fin's FATIGUE ENDPOINT -- the coordinator's reading, not Ty's, of what the wing's \"ultimate strength of 1g\" means for a surface that carries almost nothing in balanced flight: a fully fatigued fin fails the next time the pilot uses the rudder the way he always does."
  },
  "spawnIasMs": 26.8,
  "instruments": {
    "airspeed": {
      "units": "mph",
      "_unitsSource": "Era and category: 1917 fabric. mph is the only unit the aeroplane ever had.",
      "_unitsRule": "Ty 2026-11-10, asked what rule the other 27 types follow after settling five by hand, chose \"by era and category\": \"Vintage, fabric and experimental types read mph; anything certified modern or turbine reads knots.\" FlyingGame.Core.Instruments.AsiUnits carries the rule and the exceptions."
    }
  },
  "spinAero": {
    "controlSpoilerK": 0.6,
    "controlSpoilerKSource": "A deflected control on a BROADSIDE surface acts as a spoiler on the separated wake, not as a flap: adds k*sin(delta)*|sin(alpha_chord)|*q*S normal to the FIXED surface's own plane, over hinged tail strips only, ramped in over 20 deg from controlSpoilerOnsetDeg. 0.6 is the PITTS' value and is carried unchanged, not refitted per type: it was fitted there on 2026-11-10 to Ty flying a clip of his own Pitts flat spin (\"the elevator literally does nothing in the spin\"), and pitts-s2b-like.json holds the sweep it came from - elevator authority at alpha 80 of 3.7 deg/s2 at k 0, 15.5 at 0.3, 27.4 at 0.6, 43.4 at 1.0, where 1.0 leaves the curve nearly flat from 30 to 90 deg and a plate should not be. WHY THIS TYPE HAS IT NOW. It shipped on the Pitts and the Extra alone, and that was STAGING rather than a finding: the argument for the term is about the SECTION - post-stall the section law is Cd_max*sin(theta), which is flat at broadside, so a strip model gives a control on a broadside surface a direction to point its force in and no magnitude to command - and when this type's horizontal tail became a flat plate on 2026-11-10 (b7396cd0: stall 9 deg, Cl_max 0.90, blend 44) that argument started applying to it too. Worse, the controlSpoilerOnsetDeg 10 that came with the plate was INERT here, because the spoiler block in AeroModel.Compute is gated on controlSpoilerK > 0.0: the onset was moving the start of a term multiplied by zero, and the hole it was meant to close was being closed by the 44 deg blend alone. Ty 2026-11-10, shown exactly that and the argument that the restriction to two types was staging: \"Do it\". MEASURED ON THIS TYPE (SpoilerFleetProbe, SPOILER_FLEET=1). Authority is the pitching moment the stick's whole travel commands at 30 m/s with the wind turned to the body alpha asked for, as a fraction of its own alpha 0 value; the floor is the worst of it over alpha 10-60 at a 0.5 deg step. Floor 0.150 at alpha 42 -> 0.250 at alpha 28; alpha 45 0.193 -> 0.315. IT COSTS THE PITCH DAMPING NOTHING, and for a mechanical reason rather than a lucky one: the term carries sin(gain*deflEff) and is gated on a non-zero deflection, so it cannot appear in a controls-neutral quantity, and dM/dq is one. VERIFIED rather than asserted, because if it had moved then the gate would not be what we think it is: the worst dM/dq over alpha 10-60 is BIT-IDENTICAL at k 0 and k 0.6 on all eleven configs that gained it and on the two that already had it. Vso AND THE TRIM ARE UNMOVED TOO, which was not obvious and had to be measured rather than argued: unlike stabHighAlphaK's 60-90 deg band, this term opens at 10 deg of the strip's OWN flow, and a tail strip with the stick near its aft stop at the stall is not self-evidently below that. It is - measured on the Stearman at its own Vso, the stabiliser's local angle is 2.74 deg against the 10 deg onset, because the downwash (3.49 deg there) and the stabiliser's own negative incidence take it out of the body alpha - so the ramp never opens in any ordinary flight condition and every type's Vso, trim alpha and trim elevator come out unchanged to the digit. WHAT IT DOES CHANGE is high-alpha pitch authority and therefore the spin, which is the point, and nothing below the ramp.",
    "controlSpoilerOnsetDeg": 10.0,
    "controlSpoilerOnsetDegSource": "The spoiler ramp starts at the SECTION'S OWN STALL. 30 deg was chosen when every hStab was a naca0012 (stall 14 deg); a flat plate stalls at 9-12, which left alpha 20-34 with no table effectiveness and no spoiler yet - and that band WAS the hole in the elevator authority. Worth the whole difference: authority floor 0.165 at onset 30 against 0.249 at onset 10. Ty 2026-11-10 accepted the plate knowing this moves the term he judged the Pitts' spin on."
  }
